nodecode-agentwallet is a rewrite of openclawcash's openclawcash/agentwalletapi-hermes:

  https://github.com/openclawcash/agentwalletapi-hermes
  commit ae547a9c51dfc6060fe2441411f9ec846250ef75
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 OpenClawCash (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the whole of what the plugin gives a user -- its five tools
(agentwalletapi_wallets_list, _wallet_get, _wallet_rename, _wallet_create and
_transfer_send), their argument names, the five OpenClawCash agent endpoints
they call, the X-Agent-Key header the agent key travels in, the host allowlist
that keeps that key on openclawcash.com, and the plugin's own guidance about
labels being data and writes selecting a wallet by walletId -- as a Nodecode
cell. The plugin was a thin Python bridge over urllib, so nothing here is
vendored: each tool is one nlk:http exchange, the section holds the key
(inline, or the name of the environment variable holding it), and every answer
is rendered for the model as text rather than as the HTTP client's parsed
object. The plugin's docstrings and README notes are the cell's manual,
(help :agentwallet). The export passphrase stays where the plugin put it: the
OPENCLAWCASH_EXPORT_PASSPHRASE variable, read at the moment wallet_create
needs it, sent to the service that custodies the wallet, and never a tool
argument.

Left out: the plugin's refusal to follow HTTP redirects. Its client disabled
them so the key could not be resent to a host a Location header named;
nlk:http follows up to five, so the allowlist -- openclawcash.com and one
label under it, no port, path or credentials -- is the guard this cell carries.
Also left out: private-key import, which the plugin itself kept off the tool
surface so a raw key never enters the model's context, and the swap, bridge,
escrow and casino tools of the wider MCP server, which the plugin did not
expose either. The plugin's AGENTWALLETAPI_KEY and AGENTWALLETAPI_URL
fallbacks are kept, as the section's own members.
